Hi Rahul,

Taking the whole series together here (replying on 1/3 since that's where
most of the core logic lives). A few comments below, in addition to the
thread Charles already started.

1. Seconding the dead-code concern on ->readattr()
----------------------------------------------------
I agree with Charles here:

> +		/* Protocol-specific attributes */
> +		if (!chan->proto || !chan->proto->readattr)
> +			return -ENOSYS;
> +
> +		ret = sbi_ecall(SBI_EXT_MPXY, SBI_EXT_MPXY_READ_ATTRS,
> +				chan->channel_id, base_attr_id, attr_count,
> +				0, 0, 0);
> +		if (ret.error)
> +			return -EINVAL;
> +
> +		for (i = 0; i < attr_count; i++)
> +			attrs_buf[i] = le32_to_cpu(shmem[i]);

sbi_mpxy_read_attrs() checks chan->proto->readattr is non-NULL purely as a
gate, then goes ahead and does the generic ecall + shmem copy itself —
the actual rpmi_readattr() callback (patch 2/3) is never invoked anywhere.
Please either call chan->proto->readattr() here for v2, or drop the
readattr member/callback and the NULL check entirely as Charles suggested.
Shipping an unused callback plus a dead implementation (rpmi_readattr in
cmd/riscv/sbi_mpxy_cmd.c) just invites bit-rot.

2. Missing bounds check against shmem_size in message send path
------------------------------------------------------------------
sbi_mpxy_read_attrs()/write_attrs() correctly bound attr_count against
mpxy_ctx.shmem_size:

> +	if (attr_count > mpxy_ctx.shmem_size / sizeof(u32))
> +		return -EINVAL;

but sbi_mpxy_send_message_with_resp() does not apply the same discipline:

> +	if (tx_len)
> +		memcpy(shmem, tx, tx_len);
...
> +	if (rx) {
> +		rx_bytes = ret.value;
> +		if (rx_bytes > max_rx_len)
> +			return -ENOSPC;
> +		memcpy(rx, shmem, rx_bytes);

tx_len is only checked against the caller-provided max_rx_len/tx_len, never
against mpxy_ctx.shmem_size. A caller with a tx_len larger than the actual
shmem window (e.g. via sbi_mpxy_send(), which forwards up to
SBI_MPXY_MAX_MSG_WORDS = 64 words = 256 bytes regardless of the real
negotiated shmem size from SBI_EXT_MPXY_GET_SHMEM_SIZE) will overflow the
allocated shmem buffer on the memcpy(shmem, tx, tx_len) above. Likewise,
rx_bytes comes straight from firmware (ret.value) and is only capped by
max_rx_len, not by shmem_size, so a misbehaving/buggy SBI implementation
can cause an out-of-bounds read of the shmem allocation on the
memcpy(rx, shmem, rx_bytes) call. Please clamp both tx_len and rx_bytes to
mpxy_ctx.shmem_size (or expose shmem_size and check it in the command
layer) before touching the buffer.

3. No cleanup of shmem allocation on later probe failures
--------------------------------------------------------------
mpxy_setup_shmem() correctly frees the shmem buffer if SET_SHMEM fails:

> +	if (ret.error) {
> +		log_err("sbi_mpxy: SET_SHMEM failed (%ld)\n", ret.error);
> +		free((void *)(uintptr_t)mpxy_ctx.shmem_addr);
> +		mpxy_ctx.shmem_addr = 0;
> +		return -EINVAL;
> +	}

but sbi_mpxy_probe()'s own `fail:` label does not free it:

> +	ret = mpxy_scan_channels();
> +	if (ret)
> +		goto fail;
> +...
> +fail:
> +	mpxy_available = false;
> +	return ret;

If mpxy_scan_channels() or the per-channel sbi_mpxy_read_attrs() loop
fails after mpxy_setup_shmem() has already succeeded, the shmem buffer
stays allocated forever (mpxy_available is false, but shmem_addr/shmem_size
are never reset or freed), and a later retry path has no way to redo the
setup cleanly. Please free/zero mpxy_ctx.shmem_addr in the `fail:` path too.

4. Duplicate function prototypes added to arch/riscv/include/asm/sbi.h
----------------------------------------------------------------------
The patch appends a second, fully kerneldoc'd set of declarations for
functions that are already declared a few lines above in the same hunk:

> +void sbi_set_timer(uint64_t stime_value);
> +long sbi_get_spec_version(void);
> +int sbi_get_impl_id(void);
...
> +int sbi_get_mvendorid(long *mvendorid);
> +int sbi_get_marchid(long *marchid);
> +int sbi_get_mimpid(long *mimpid);
> +void sbi_srst_reset(unsigned long type, unsigned long reason);
> +int sbi_dbcn_write_byte(unsigned char ch);

sbi_set_timer(), sbi_get_spec_version(), sbi_get_impl_id(),
sbi_get_mvendorid(), sbi_get_marchid(), sbi_get_mimpid(), sbi_srst_reset()
and sbi_dbcn_write_byte() were all already declared a few lines earlier
(pre-existing code, lines just above the new MPXY additions). This looks
like leftover from rebasing/reformatting the header with doc comments.
Please drop the duplicate block and only add the genuinely new MPXY
prototypes (sbi_mpxy_register_proto, sbi_mpxy_probe, etc.).

Also minor: `#include <compiler.h>` is added to sbi.h but I don't see
anything in this patch that needs it (no BIT()/compiler-attribute usage
in sbi.h itself) — worth double-checking it's not a leftover unused
include.

Overall: needs a v2 addressing the above before this can be merged.

Thanks,
Leo
